From c5e9cf86c749c2a0777aa0813697a673a9c49898 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:35:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08061/11362] [outer-loop] security audit fix 5/5: re-baseline
 gitleaks, and close two blind spots

The header of .gitleaks.toml recorded a clean full-history scan at 2,542
commits. The repo is at 21,323 and nobody had re-run it in the ~18,800 since.
The audit's run produced 544 findings -- all rule `generic-api-key`, all
verified benign by extracting each matched line from its git object:

  378  {"key": "<32-hex>", ...} content-hash cache keys in detector JSONL
  103  snake_case verdict/outcome enums in result artifacts
    3  reproducibility_checksum digests
   60  prose, HTTP response captures, an Ed25519PrivateKey type annotation

Zero real secrets, but 544 is a report nobody reads, which is a security problem
in itself. Allowlisted the 378-finding cache class by path. Residual ~166, all
reviewed.

TWO BLIND SPOTS FOUND WHILE VALIDATING THE ALLOWLIST, both now closed:

1. gitleaks 8.30.1 has NO rule for Anthropic `sk-ant-` or OpenAI `sk-proj-`
   keys, and generic-api-key catches neither. Confirmed against a STOCK
   useDefault config, so this was never a side effect of our allowlists. For a
   repo developed with Claude Code that declares an `openai` extra, those were
   the two likeliest credentials to leak. Custom rules added -- and they
   immediately proved themselves by making the pre-commit hook refuse this very
   commit over the planted keys in the new probe file.

2. A second allowlist scoping benign value shapes to results/docs/openspec
   applied REPO-WIDE. gitleaks ORs allowlist conditions, and the documented fix
   `matchCondition = "AND"` SILENTLY NO-OPS in this build -- an isolation test
   with one allowed/ and one blocked/ path showed both suppressed, with no
   warning emitted. That allowlist is NOT shipped: a Kaggle API key is 32
   lowercase hex characters and this repo holds Kaggle credentials, so it would
   have blinded the scanner to a leaked Kaggle key in any file to buy a tidier
   report. It cost ~106 more suppressions and was not worth it. The rejection
   and its reasoning are recorded in the config so it is not re-attempted.

tests/python/test_gitleaks_config.py locks all of it in. Two notes on the tests:

- Every scan carries a POSITIVE CONTROL. The first version invoked
  `gitleaks detect --source <path>`, a legacy form this build accepts and
  IGNORES; it scanned nothing, and two "must be suppressed" assertions passed
  vacuously on an empty result. The correct invocation is `gitleaks dir`.
- test_hex_credential_in_source_tree_is_detected was mutation-proven: re-adding
  the rejected allowlist makes it fail, so it genuinely bites rather than
  passing by construction.

Adds REQ-SEC-002.
